{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["And he said: <b>Let my soul die the death of the upright.</b> This is in the plural, while 'let my end be like his' is in the singular — for death applies specifically to the body that comes from father and mother, which he called 'upright' (in the plural, corresponding to the two parents). But 'let my end be like his' — for the afterlife and enduring survival belong to the souls that come from the source of HaShem, blessed be He, and the soul resembles the Creator in five things (Berakhot 10a); therefore it says 'like his (כָּמֹהוּ),' in the singular, corresponding to the one Source. And the Riv'a explained that the final letters of אַבְרָהָם יִצְחָק יַעֲקֹב — i.e., ם ק ב — spell בִּלְעָם in gematria, and this is the meaning of 'my end (אַחֲרִיתִי) like his (כָּמֹהוּ),' for the end-letters point to Balaam."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The text as given gives the Riv'a's explanation as a gematria of the final letters (ם ק ב) of the three patriarchs' names equaling בִּלְעָם; the word אַחֲרִיתִי ('my end') is read as a pun on the end-letters of the names."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:49.992730893Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}